## 0140 — Home as stages held on the window

- **Date:** 2026-09-28 to 2026-09-29
- **Direction:** Timothy on Home (Q41): “its just kind of flat and basic. the style is strong and it works. but at the end of the day its just a really long flat page”; keep the CLI/terminal aesthetic but add depth, “as if the page stays static, but the content moves as you scroll”, then “not just the selected works. the whole page.” Three whole-page studies (a tunnel of planes, a deck, a terminal redraw) were rejected: the deck was closer, but “i dont want it to look like planes just animating away. that animation to me feels cheap. i want it to feel like a terminal.” Then: “start from scratch again. each section can have its own thing.” For Selected work: a vertical carousel with the title and blurb on the left and the image changing as you scroll; the cards “arranged kind of like theyre on a barrell”, but not turning (“it should just be scaling and opacity”); “the ios picker is what i mean”; the drum tighter, no card cropped, the copy level with the card; the rain behind kept but “more faded and less distracting”; a lowered frame rate “isnt the effect”; the scanlines in the ground colour “so it appears to fade in and out”, and more pronounced. Then “now the rest of the page”, and for the statement: “let the statement type itself once pinned.” On charmbracelet/harmonica: “can we leverage this at all?” Shown the result on a dev-only mock, he said: “commit it and push it.”
- **Implementation (PX-54):** Home is four stages, each a window-sized sticky frame in a taller section (`src/lib/components/home/`). The hero is the 0128 terminal hero; scrolling sends its lines out through scanlines, redraws the name in the sky's own glyphs, sinks them into the field a cell at a time and fills the window with the panel colour in lines. The statement, once held, types its sentence behind the cursor, scrambles in the three words with their chips and prints the companion a line at a time; it plays once, and leaving finishes it. Selected work is a picker wheel: the cards ride a 40° drum and never turn, scaled by a fixed perspective and faded into the ground by 4px scanlines of the ground colour, all snapped to the 8px unit; the copy is level with a yellow selection frame; the scroll picks the nearest card and a damped spring (ω 11, ζ 0.72, ported from harmonica) carries the drum there, so it lands with one 8px step of give. The invitation brings the rain up, stacks the drops into “building.” in glyphs from the bottom row, and resolves the drawn word over them. Each stage after the hero overlaps the one before by a window and is clear until held, so every handoff meets on the same ground and nothing slides between stages. Motion off, no JavaScript and the prerender show plain sections, the wheel as a list of the four projects.
- **Assistant proposals inside it:** the hero's dissolve and the fill into the panel, the invitation's drops, the statement's order and timing, the overlapping handoffs, the spring's detents and constants, the scanline look over the 1-bit look it was shown against, and the motion-off list. The old Work heading (“Products, front ends, and brand systems. Here’s some of my work.”) left with the old section; the wheel's copy replaces it.
- **Status:** Timothy's direction; shipped on his word on 2026-09-29 after reviewing the mock. He has not commented on the hero, statement and invitation treatments or the spring one by one. Resolves Q41. Evidence in `docs/iterations/pixel-v2/54-home-stages/`.


